Package: usb-lcd-dashboard
Version: @VERSION@
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Maintainer: USB LCD Dashboard <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Depends: python3 (>= 3.12), python3-pil (>= 10.1), python3-serial (>= 3.5),
 python3-usb (>= 1.2.1), python3-pycryptodome (>= 3.20), python3-gi,
 gir1.2-ayatanaappindicator3-0.1, python3-numpy, python3-psutil,
 fonts-dejavu-core
Recommends: systemd, git, fonts-ubuntu, xdg-desktop-portal
Homepage: https://github.com/smartguy05/usb-lcd
Description: Live Claude Code and Codex dashboard for a USB LCD panel
 Drives a Turing/UsbMonitor 3.5-inch USB LCD as a status display for Claude
 Code and Codex sessions, showing the provider, model, project, elapsed time,
 permission requests and context used. An optional Discord bot integration
 displays new messages from selected server channels.
 .
 The panel is divided into tiles, each holding a widget: a clock, an agent
 card, an animated crab, the latest selected-channel Discord message, or a
 persistent human todo list shared with Claude Code and Codex, or Claude
 subscription usage and reset times.
 .
 Installing this package puts the program and its udev rule in place. Wiring it
 into your own Claude and Codex sessions is a per-user step: run
 "usb-lcd-dashboard install" as yourself, which merges the hooks and enables a
 systemd user service.
 .
 fonts-dejavu-core is a hard dependency rather than a recommendation: a minimal
 Ubuntu ships no TrueType font at all, and the panel would silently fall back to
 a bitmap face on a device whose whole purpose is legible text.
 .
 python3-usb and python3-pycryptodome drive the current-generation TURZX panels,
 which speak native USB with encrypted commands rather than the 3.5-inch panel's
 serial protocol. The versions asked for here are the ones Ubuntu ships, which
 sit below the floors in pyproject.toml: those floors came in with a bulk
 dependency bump, and every call this package makes -- usb.util.find_descriptor,
 endpoint read/write, and DES in CBC mode -- long predates both.
 .
 python3-gi and gir1.2-ayatanaappindicator3-0.1 provide the notification-area
 icon, which is how the settings editor is reached on a desktop. Both are
 listed as hard dependencies to keep a default install identical to Windows;
 on a headless box they cost a few megabytes and the daemon logs that it found
 no tray and carries on.
 .
 xdg-desktop-portal is only a recommendation because a headless install needs
 nothing of it, but on a desktop it is what makes the tray menu work: the
 daemon runs as a sandboxed systemd user unit, so it cannot start a browser or
 a file manager itself, and the portal opens them in the session instead.
 Without it the menu items fall back to xdg-open, which under that sandbox
 exits successfully and opens nothing.
